By default (\code{interference = NULL}, with the option below unset) crossovers
follow a two-pathway gamma model of interference with \code{nu = 2.6} and
\code{p = 0}: AlphaSimR's default (its \code{SimParam} fields \code{v} and \code{p}), which
approximates Kosambi's map function. Give \code{interference = list(nu = , p = )}
for other values, or \code{interference = "poisson"} for Poisson crossovers (no
interference), whose random draws are exactly isqg's. Before version
2.0.0.9003 Poisson was the default; seeded results of every function that runs
meiosis changed with the new default (see \code{NEWS.md}). The gamma model:
\itemize{
\item The four-strand bivalent carries chiasmata at an average of 2 per Morgan.
A gamete takes part in each chiasma independently with probability 1/2
(no chromatid interference), so it carries one crossover per Morgan on
average: \strong{the genetic map (\code{cm}) keeps its meaning for every \code{nu} and
\code{p}}, as under Haldane.
\item A share \code{p} of the chiasmata (intensity \eqn{2p} per Morgan) is
non-interfering: a Poisson process.
\item The rest (intensity \eqn{2(1 - p)}) is interfering: a stationary renewal
process whose gaps are gamma distributed with shape \code{nu} and rate
\eqn{2 \nu (1 - p)} per Morgan (mean gap \eqn{1 / (2 (1 - p))} Morgans).
\verb{1 <= nu <= 1e6} is the interference strength (larger values are rejected:
the gamma gaps are then numerically a fixed spacing); \code{nu = 1} is
exponential gaps, i.e. no interference. The first chiasma is drawn from the stationary
(equilibrium) distribution, so the process does not "start" at the
chromosome end.
\item The two pathways are independent; the gamete's crossovers are the union.
}

The recombination fraction between two loci \eqn{d} Morgans apart is then
\eqn{r(d) = [1 - P_0(d)] / 2}, with \eqn{P_0(d)} the probability of no
chiasma in the interval: \eqn{P_0(d) = e^{-2pd} \{1 - F_{\nu+1}(y) -
